Subject: Re: [r-inla] Is there a legitimate shortcut for computing post-hoc linear combinations that doesn't involve enormous posterior samples?
Message-ID: <aOnPXqbIt74.qFrGM3MNFgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <aOnPXqbIt74.EiCkxBENFgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <aOnPXqbIt74.U0ZwQ-jxFQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <aOnPXqbIt74.EiCkxBENFgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/aOnPXqbIt74/m/qFrGM3MNFgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

...and a different approach is to use the config information not to
generate samples, but to replicate the final step of the INLA
approximations in separate R code tailored for your specific linear
combinations (probably mainly useful for mean and variance of linear
combinations, and requires understanding of how to efficiently compute
variances of linear combinations from expectations and precisions. The
raw basics can be seen here:
https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~flindgre/cuso2019/gmrf.pdf

Finn

On Fri, 7 Feb 2020 at 11:28, Finn Lindgren <finn.l...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Hi Tim,
>
> to add to Haakon's suggestions, item 1. can be skipped if you have
> space to store all the configs (which I think you already do).
> The configs (that store the information about each numerical
> integration component for the posterior mixture distribution arising
> from the computations) are used by inla.posterior.sample. In the
> "excursions" package, we use them for a sampling method tailored to
> computing posterior event probabilities, in a way that can take
> skewness (and other) corrections into account, that I believe the
> current inla.posterior.sample implementation doesn't (but this may get
> implemented in the future).
>
> However, none of that really solves the issue of needing to store lots
> and lots of samples.
> The good news is that there are partial solutions available, and more
> coming in the future.
> 1. For posterior means and variances, you don't need access to all the
> samples at the same time; you can instead accumulate the needed
> information from a sequence of sample batches, resulting in the same
> result as when first generating all the samples and then summarising
> them.
> 2. For quantiles, you could use sample batches and apply a recursive
> quantile estimator. This will not be as accurate (for a given sample
> size) as quantiles from the full sample, but it massively reduces the
> storage requirements; by producing more samples in total, you
> effectively trade storage space for computation time.
> 3. One of my ongoing projects is a Bayesian improvement of the
> recursive technique from 2.
>
> Finn
>
> On Fri, 7 Feb 2020 at 09:22, Haakon Bakka <ba...@r-inla.org> wrote:
> >
> > Hi Tim,
> >
> > This might not be a direct answer to your questions, but I can suggest a way to approach these problems.
> >
> > 1. Running INLA with int.strategy="eb", and the option to store the config
> > 2. The latent posterior can be completely represented by the internal mode of the hyper-parameters, and the single precision matrix found in config
> > 3. Store all the hyperpars and precision matrices across all models
> > 4. Use the precision matrix of each model to generate samples when needed
> >
