formf= y ~  -1 + Intercept +pond.std+ f(w, model=spde, hyper=hyperspde1)+f(w.group, model="ar1", hyper=hyper.ar1)

MF <- inla(formf, family="poisson",
data= inla.stack.data(stk.full), verbose= TRUE,
control.compute= list(config=TRUE, cpo=TRUE, waic=TRUE),
control.inla= list(strategy="adaptive", npoints = 21,
int.strategy="eb",cmin=0, diagonal=0),
control.fixed= list(mean.intercept=0, prec.intercept=0.001),
control.predictor=list(link=1, A=inla.stack.A(stk.full), compute=TRUE))

where the mesh$n = 27,863.

Using posterior sampling methods suggested by Fuglstad and Beguin (2017: Environmental mapping using Bayesian spatial modeling(INLA/SPDE): A reply to Huang et al. 2017) I developed annual predictions for my study region for each of 10 years. Cross-checking the results against previous publications and actual data, the predictions make 'sense' with the exception of the first year which is inflated to over twice what I would reasonably expect it to be.

Is this an artifact typical of an AR(1) model? If so are there ways to address it? and if not, does anyone have any ideas about what could be causing this issue from a modeling standpoint?

Many thanks,
KK

